You have at least as much control on the console, using loadkeys.
(Don't you need to restart X for the changes to take effect? On console,
the changes are immediate, no need for logout.)
For example, I don't like some key selections on the default Finnish
keyboard, so I have added the command "loadkeys /etc/jlkeys.map" to my
/etc/rc.local and the file /etc/jlkeys.map looks like this:
keycode 5 = four dollar
keycode 13 = at grave
keycode 41 = asciitilde asciicircum

These settings change shift-4 to dollar sign (it is the asshole sign in
the default Finnish keymap), the key left of the backspace key to send the
at sign (@) when pressed alone and grave accent (`) with shift, and the
key left of '1' at the top row to send tilde (~) alone and circumflex (^)
with shift.

You can use loadkeys either to change the whole keymap or just the
individual keys you want to change, and you can add the command to the
nethack starting script to change the keys before launching the game
itself and then change them back when you exit the game.
But the changes will also affect other consoles while the game is running
on one, and I don't remember whether you need root privileges to use it.
See the manpages of loadkeys and keymaps for more information. 
You can use the command showkeys to check the keycodes and dumpkeys to
output the current settings.

